+          # "Kafka broker was OOM-killed today") -- triggered the
+          # broker restart that disrupted the Frente 3 reprocess
+          # session's resume. RSS was already back up to ~528MiB
+          # (~69%) within a minute of that restart, and observed at
+          # 585MiB (~76%) again under the reprocess's steady load a
+          # few hours later -- consistent with 768M genuinely being
+          # tight for this workload, not a one-off fluke.
+          #
+          # PROPOSED, NOT DECIDED UNILATERALLY: raising to 1024M
+          # (the same relative first step bronze-consumer's own
+          # 512M->1024M->1536M history took, not a number picked in a
+          # vacuum) -- but unlike bronze-consumer's raises, this is
+          # NOT free headroom. Checked live before proposing any
+          # number (`free -h`, same session): host has only ~262MiB
+          # truly free, ~2.5GiB "available" (kernel's own reclaimable
+          # estimate), and swap already at ~3.7GiB/6GiB (62%) in use --
+          # this matches the still-open "Host RAM stays under real
+          # pressure with the full local stack running" roadmap entry
+          # (every container's *individual* ceiling already summed to
+          # ~13GB+ before this proposed change, against an 8GB+4GB-swap
+          # host budget). Raising this container's ceiling doesn't
+          # create new host RAM -- it lets Kafka claim more of an
+          # already-oversubscribed shared pool before ITS OWN cgroup
+          # OOM-kills it, which could just as easily show up as *more*
+          # swap thrashing or the kernel's host-level (not per-cgroup)
+          # OOM killer picking a different victim instead (this
+          # session's own `dmesg` capture from the same OOM event
